<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles Magoon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1861">1861</a>–<a href="/years/1920">1920</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Charles Edward Magoon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles Magoon was an American lawyer and administrator who had governed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama-canal-zone">Panama Canal Zone</a>. In 1906 he replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-howard-taft">William Howard Taft</a> as provisional governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> during the second American occupation and ruled until 1909. He kept Cuban laws and flag, built roads and distributed government posts to pacify factions, and was blamed by Cubans for corruption.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-magoon.jpg" alt="Charles Magoon" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Harris &amp; Ewing · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CharlesEMagoon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Magoon was born in Owatonna, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minnesota">Minnesota</a>, on 5 December 1861, and his family moved to Nebraska while he was a small child. He studied at the University of Nebraska from 1876, left in 1879 to read law with a prominent firm, was admitted to the bar in 1882 and became a partner in Lincoln. As judge advocate of the Nebraska National Guard he took the title of Judge, which he kept for the rest of his career.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1899 he joined the law office of the War Department's new Division of Customs and Insular Affairs. A report he prepared for Secretary Russell A. Alger in May 1899 held that the Constitution follows the flag into the territories taken after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish-American War</a>; under Elihu Root a second report, of 12 February 1900, reached the opposite conclusion, a view the Supreme Court largely adopted in the Insular Cases from 1901. A leak of the first report during the debate on the Puerto Rico Tariff Act became the Magoon Incident. His reports, published as a book in 1902, were long considered the standard work on the subject.</p><p>In June 1904 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-roosevelt">Theodore Roosevelt</a> made him general counsel of the Isthmian Canal Commission, and in 1905 governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama-canal-zone">Panama Canal Zone</a>, a member of the commission and, from 2 July, also Minister to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a>, drawing two salaries. He came to accept that mosquitoes carried yellow fever and malaria and set out to drain the swamps that bred them. After Congress barred a diplomat from holding another post, he was named governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> instead.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Magoon took office in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> on 13 October 1906 without an inauguration, declaring that he would preserve Cuban independence. Under him about two hundred kilometres of highway were built and he called for a formal Cuban army and the removal of the wreck of the USS Maine from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a> harbour. On 28 January 1909 Cuban government was restored under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-miguel-gomez">José Miguel Gómez</a>. Admired in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and commended by President Taft, he was bitterly criticised by Cuban writers: the bibliographer Carlos Manuel Trelles wrote that he had profoundly corrupted the nation, though no explicit evidence of his corruption ever surfaced. He retired from public life and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a>, on 14 January 1920 after surgery for appendicitis.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charles Edward Magoon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Taft was soon replaced by <b>Charles Magoon</b>, who governed until 1909 while preserving Cuba's flag and laws.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-cuba">Provisional Government of Cuba</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Owatonna, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.08,-93.23&amp;year=1899" aria-label="Owatonna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2316 603 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Charles Magoon"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2766 873h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2766 873h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.08,-93.23&amp;year=1899">What was here in 1899? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
